      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need a different sealer for my basement floor than my basement walls?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Generally yes. Walls face lateral hydrostatic pressure from saturated soil and are usually addressed with masonry waterproofing paints or penetrating sealers designed for vertical surfaces — see our basement wall sealer guide. Floors face upward moisture vapor transmission and foot traffic, which calls for either a penetrating densifier or a film-forming epoxy rated for the floor's moisture level. Some products, like RadonSeal, work on both surfaces, but most wall-specific waterproofing paints aren't formulated or warrantied for floor use.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I know if my basement floor needs a vapor barrier sealer instead of standard epoxy?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Run the plastic sheet moisture test described above. If the underside shows visible condensation or pooling after 24 hours, or if a professional calcium chloride/RH test comes back elevated, a standard epoxy is likely to bubble and delaminate. Choose a moisture-tolerant epoxy or a dedicated vapor barrier primer system instead — the extra cost is far cheaper than redoing a failed floor coating.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I put epoxy over a penetrating densifier?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, and it's a reasonable combination strategy for basements that will eventually become finished space. Apply the densifier first to harden and seal the concrete's internal pore structure, let it cure fully per label instructions, then etch or lightly grind the surface for adhesion before applying your epoxy topcoat. Just confirm your specific epoxy manufacturer doesn't prohibit application over a densified surface — most don't, but check the data sheet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will sealing the floor fix a musty basement smell?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can help significantly if the smell is coming from moisture wicking up through the slab, since that moisture feeds mold and mildew growth. It won't fix musty odors caused by high ambient humidity from poor ventilation (address with a dehumidifier), an active leak elsewhere (address with drainage or crack repair first), or existing mold in porous materials like drywall or carpet padding, which needs to be removed rather than sealed over.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
