                <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Basement Floor Sealing Mistakes
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    A basement slab sits on damp ground with no way to dry downward, which makes it the least forgiving concrete in the house to seal. Nearly every failure comes back to moisture.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Without Testing for Moisture First</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Vapour rising through a slab will push almost any film-forming sealer back off within months, and you cannot judge it by eye. Tape a two-foot square of plastic sheet down tightly and leave it 24 to 48 hours: condensation underneath means the slab is passing moisture and needs a vapour-tolerant product or a vapour barrier, not a standard sealer.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an Exterior Film Sealer Indoors</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Many solvent-based acrylics and driveway sealers give off enough vapour to make an enclosed basement genuinely unsafe to work in, and they trap moisture besides. In a basement, use a product rated for interior use with adequate ventilation, and favour a breathable penetrating sealer where the slab is damp.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Instead of Fixing the Water Source</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Sealer is a finish, not a waterproofing system. If water is entering through wall-floor joints, cracks, or hydrostatic pressure from below, coating the floor moves the problem rather than solving it. Sort the gutters, grading, and any active leaks first, then seal.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Etching or Grinding on a Slick Slab</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Older basement slabs are often troweled to a hard, closed surface that sealer cannot key into. Applied over it, the coating looks fine until the first thing scrapes across it and lifts a sheet. Acid-etch or mechanically grind the surface to an open profile before anything goes down.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Closing -->
                <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
                  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    A <strong class="text-white">basement floor sealer</strong> only works when the slab is dry enough to hold it. Run a plastic-sheet moisture test first, because it decides everything else: a dry slab takes almost any interior-rated product, while a slab passing vapour needs a breathable penetrating sealer or a proper vapour barrier system.
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                    Fix active water entry before you seal, prepare the surface until it is open and porous, and choose a product rated for interior use. Done in that order, a sealed basement floor stays dry, cleans easily, and stops the slab from feeding humidity into the rest of the house.
                  </p>
                </div>

                <div class="mt-16 text-center bg-blue-50 p-12 rounded-3xl border border-blue-100 shadow-inner">
                    <h3 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                        Test the Slab. Match the Sealer. Protect It.
                    </h3>
                    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 mb-8 max-w-xl mx-auto">
                        A basement floor sealer is one of the cheapest, highest-return home improvement products you can buy — but only if you match it to how wet your slab actually is. Run the plastic sheet test, pick the right category, and prep the surface properly. Skip any one of those three and even the best product on this list will fail.
                    </p>
